DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The jjaaiill library is an interface to the jail_set(2) and jail_get(2) sys‐
     tem calls, and the _s_e_c_u_r_i_t_y_._j_a_i_l_._p_a_r_a_m MIB entries.  It simplifies the
     conversion of prison parameters between internal and string formats, al‐
     lowing the setting and querying of prisons without knowing the parameter
     formats.

     The jjaaiill__ggeettiidd() function returns the JID of the jail identified by _n_a_m_e,
     or -1 if the jail does not exist.

     The jjaaiill__ggeettnnaammee() function returns the name of the jail identified by
     _j_i_d, or NULL if the jail does not exist.

     The jjaaiill__sseettvv() function takes a null-terminated list of name and value
     strings, and passes it to jail_set(2).

     The jjaaiill__ggeettvv() function takes a null-terminated list of name and value
     strings, and passes it to jail_get(2).  It is the caller's responsibility
     to ensure that the value strings point to buffers large enough to hold
     the string representation of the returned parameters.

     The jjaaiillppaarraamm__aallll() function sets _j_p_p to a list of all known jail parame‐
     ters, and returns the number of parameters.  The list should later be
     freed with jjaaiillppaarraamm__ffrreeee() and free(3).

     The jjaaiillppaarraamm__iinniitt() function clears a parameter record and copies the
     _n_a_m_e to it.  After use, it should be freed with jjaaiillppaarraamm__ffrreeee().

     The jjaaiillppaarraamm__iimmppoorrtt() function adds a _v_a_l_u_e to a parameter record, con‐
     verting it from a string to its native form.  The jjaaiillppaarraamm__iimmppoorrtt__rraaww()
     function adds a value without performing any conversion.

     The jjaaiillppaarraamm__sseett() function passes a list of parameters to jail_set(2).
     The parameters are assumed to have been created with jjaaiillppaarraamm__iinniitt() and
     jjaaiillppaarraamm__iimmppoorrtt().

     The jjaaiillppaarraamm__ggeett() function passes a list of parameters to jail_get(2).
     The parameters are assumed to have been created with jjaaiillppaarraamm__iinniitt() or
     jjaaiillppaarraamm__lliisstt(), with one parameter (the key) having been given a value
     with jjaaiillppaarraamm__iimmppoorrtt().

     The jjaaiillppaarraamm__eexxppoorrtt() function returns the string equivalent of a param‐
     eter value.  The returned string should be freed after use.

     The jjaaiillppaarraamm__ffrreeee() function frees the stored names and values in a pa‐
     rameter list.  If the list itself came from jjaaiillppaarraamm__aallll(), it should be
     freed as well.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The jjaaiill__ggeettiidd(), jjaaiill__sseettvv(), jjaaiill__ggeettvv(), jjaaiillppaarraamm__sseett() and
     jjaaiillppaarraamm__ggeett() functions return a JID on success, or -1 on error.

     The jjaaiill__ggeettnnaammee() and jjaaiillppaarraamm__eexxppoorrtt() functions return a dynamically
     allocated string on success, or NULL on error.

     The jjaaiillppaarraamm__aallll() function returns the number of parameters on success,
     or -1 on error.

     The jjaaiillppaarraamm__iinniitt(), jjaaiillppaarraamm__iimmppoorrtt() and jjaaiillppaarraamm__iimmppoorrtt__rraaww() func‐
     tions return 0 on success, or -1 on error.

     Whenever an error is returned, _e_r_r_n_o is set, and the global string
     _j_a_i_l___e_r_r_m_s_g contains a description of the error, possibly from
     jail_set(2) or jail_get(2).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Set the hostname of jail “foo” to “foo.bar”:

           jail_setv(JAIL_UPDATE, "name", "foo", "host.hostname", "foo.bar",
               NULL);

     OR:

           struct jailparam params[2];
           jailparam_init(&params[0], "name");
           jailparam_import(&params[0], "foo");
           jailparam_init(&params[1], "host.hostname");
           jailparam_import(&params[1], "foo.bar");
           jailparam_set(params, 2, JAIL_UPDATE);
           jailparam_free(params, 2);

     Retrieve the hostname of jail “foo”:

           char hostname[MAXHOSTNAMELEN];
           jail_getv(0, "name", "foo", "host.hostname", hostname, NULL);

     OR:

           struct jailparam params[2];
           jailparam_init(&params[0], "name");
           jailparam_import(&params[0], "foo");
           jailparam_init(&params[1], "host.hostname");
           jailparam_get(params, 2, 0);
           hostname = jailparam_export(&params[1]);
           jailparam_free(params, 2);
           ...
           free(hostname);

EERRRROORRSS
     The jjaaiill functions may return errors from jail_set(2), jail_get(2),
     malloc(3) or sysctl(3).  In addition, the following errors are possible:

     [EINVAL]           A parameter value cannot be converted from the passed
                        string to its internal form.

     [ENOENT]           The named parameter does not exist.

     [ENOENT]           A parameter is of an unknown type.

